/*
Problem: Count Hills and Valleys in an Array (LC 2210)
Topic: Array / Simulation

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

Consecutive duplicate values do not
affect whether an element is a hill
or valley.

First remove consecutive duplicates.

Then, for every middle element:

• Hill:
  greater than both neighbors.

• Valley:
  smaller than both neighbors.

Count all such elements.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Create a new array containing only
   distinct consecutive values.

2. Traverse the new array from the
   second element to the second-last.

3. If the current element is:

   • Greater than both neighbors,
     increment count.

   • Smaller than both neighbors,
     increment count.

4. Return the count.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

[2,4,1,1,6,5]

After removing duplicates:

[2,4,1,6,5]

Hills/Valleys:

4 → Hill

1 → Valley

6 → Hill

Answer:

3

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

[6,6,5,5,4,1]

After removing duplicates:

[6,5,4,1]

No hills or valleys.

Answer:

0

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

Consecutive duplicates belong to the
same plateau and should be treated as
a single value.

After removing them, every remaining
middle element can be checked using
its immediate neighbors.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

One pass to remove duplicates and
one pass to count hills/valleys.

Space : O(n)

Extra array stores the compressed
sequence.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Array Simulation
*/

class Solution {
public:
    int countHillValley(vector<int>& nums) {

        vector<int> arr;

        // Remove consecutive duplicates
        arr.push_back(nums[0]);

        for (int i = 1; i < nums.size(); i++) {

            if (nums[i] != nums[i - 1]) {

                arr.push_back(nums[i]);
            }
        }

        int count = 0;

        // Count hills and valleys
        for (int i = 1;
             i < arr.size() - 1;
             i++) {

            if (arr[i] > arr[i - 1] &&
                arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {

                count++;
            }

            else if (arr[i] < arr[i - 1] &&
                     arr[i] < arr[i + 1]) {

                count++;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
};
